SHOW_MSG replaces the message on screen instead of queueing behind it

Allium hands its in-game volume and brightness indicator to RetroArch over the network command
port, because RetroArch draws OSD text inside the frame it presents -- in the flip callback, after
the frame blit and before the page flip -- and so cannot flicker, where stamping the same pixels
into the framebuffer from outside always races the game's next frame.

Upstream's SHOW_MSG pushes with flush=false: each message waits its full 180 frames before the
next shows. Under a held volume key that fills the queue with stale values in a quarter of a
second. RetroArch's own volume hotkey pushes with flush=true so the newest value shows at once;
this makes SHOW_MSG behave the same way. Nothing else on the device sends it.

--- a/command.c
+++ b/command.c
@@ -732,7 +732,7 @@
 
 bool command_show_osd_msg(command_t *cmd, const char* arg)
 {
-    runloop_msg_queue_push(arg, strlen(arg), 1, 180, false, NULL,
+    runloop_msg_queue_push(arg, strlen(arg), 1, 180, true, NULL,
           MESSAGE_QUEUE_ICON_DEFAULT, MESSAGE_QUEUE_CATEGORY_INFO);
     return true;
 }
